# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Per-lesson supplements: FAQ / common pitfalls / social work connections (appended to the end of the "Intuition" section)
def FAQ(items):
    h = '<h3>Common questions and pitfalls</h3>'
    for q, a in items:
        h += f'<details class="ans faq"><summary>{q}</summary>{a}</details>'
    return h
FAQS = {}
FAQS["5.2"] = [
("How do you put the difference between between-person and within-person in one sentence?", "<p>Between-person asks: \"Are people who are lonelier <b>than others</b> also more depressed than others?\" Within-person asks: \"When someone is lonelier <b>than usual for them</b>, are they more depressed afterwards?\" The first compares different people; the second compares the same person's states at different times. Social work interventions aim at change within individuals, so we usually care more about the within-person question.</p>"),
("If the between-person correlation is positive, must the within-person correlation be positive too?", "<p>No. In the interactive figures of Lessons 4.1 and 5.3 you can set the two in opposite directions. A classic example: people who exercise regularly have lower resting heart rates overall (between-person: more exercise ↔ lower heart rate), but while a given person is exercising, their heart rate is higher than usual (within-person: more exercise ↔ higher heart rate). Be very careful when you move from the group level to the individual level, or you commit the \"ecological fallacy\".</p>"),
("Social Work Connection", "<p>Agency evaluation reports often say: \"Older adults with higher loneliness scores are also more depressed, so we should reduce loneliness.\" This sentence mixes two levels. A more accurate version: \"The two are correlated across people; whether reducing a particular older adult's loneliness would lower their later depression needs within-person evidence or an intervention study.\"</p>")]
FAQS["5.3"] = [
("Doesn't the CLPM already control for T1 through the autoregressive paths? Why do stable traits still leak into the cross-lagged paths?", "<p>Because the CLPM controls for the T1 <b>observed score</b>, and observed score = stable trait + this occasion's state (plus measurement error). Controlling for a score that only partly reflects the trait absorbs only part of the trait, much like \"controlling\" for a construct with one noisy item. The part of the trait that is not absorbed still affects both T2 loneliness and T2 depression. If the loneliness trait and the depression trait are correlated, T1 loneliness predicts T2 depression through this channel, and it shows up as a cross-lagged path. The larger the share of state in the observed score and the stronger the correlation between traits, the more leaks in. The random intercepts of the RI-CLPM model the traits separately and put the cross-lagged paths only between the within-person components, which cuts this channel (provided the traits do not change over time).</p>")]
FAQS["5.4"] = [
("How many waves does a CLPN need?", "<p>Two waves are enough to estimate one T1→T2 network. Three or more let you check replication (T2→T3) or use more complex models. Separating between-person and within-person effects usually needs more waves.</p>"),
("Social Work Connection", "<p>When you design a longitudinal study, first ask yourself: roughly how long does the change I care about take? If an intervention shows its effect within a few weeks, measuring once every six months may miss it.</p>")]
FAQS["6.1"] = [
("Do the \"nodes\" of a network have to be items?", "<p>No. A node can be a single item, a subscale or a total score, or a behavior or an environmental variable. Psychological network studies usually use single symptom items as nodes, because \"symptoms interact with each other\" is the core idea of network theory (Borsboom & Cramer, 2013). The cost is that single items are less reliable, which belongs in the limitations.</p>")]
FAQS["6.2"] = [
("Why use the precision matrix instead of simply running many regressions?", "<p>The two are mathematically equivalent: the elements of the precision matrix, converted to partial correlations, agree with regressing each node on the other variables in turn. The advantage of the precision matrix is that you get all partial correlations in one step, and it is easy to add a LASSO penalty to it directly (the graphical lasso of the next lesson).</p>")]
FAQS["6.3"] = [
("What happens to EBICglasso when the sample is small?", "<p>With a small sample the penalty weighs relatively more, so the network is very sparse: only the few strongest edges remain, or none at all. You then cannot say \"the symptoms are unrelated\", only \"the sample was too small to detect it\". The tutorial by Epskamp and Fried (2018) discusses the effect of sample size.</p>")]
FAQS["6.4"] = [
("What is the difference between <code>theme</code> and <code>palette</code>?", "<p>They control different things. <code>theme</code> is a whole set of appearance settings, the most important being the color of the <b>edges</b>: <code>theme = \"colorblind\"</code> draws positive edges blue and negative edges red, and also switches to colorblind-friendly colors for node groups. <code>palette</code> only sets the fill colors of <b>nodes</b> grouped by <code>groups</code>; it does not change edge colors. If you plot directly with <code>qgraph()</code> and only write <code>palette = \"colorblind\"</code>, the node colors change but positive edges stay green, as in qgraph's classic theme. bootnet's <code>plot()</code> already defaults to <code>theme = \"colorblind\"</code>, so adding <code>palette</code> on top of it in this lesson's code causes no conflict. In the caption, describe the edge colors actually shown: \"blue = positive\" or \"green = positive\".</p>")]
FAQS["6.5"] = [
("What should I watch for when a centrality plot uses z-scores?", "<p>z-scores let you put different indices in one plot and compare relative rankings, but they magnify tiny differences: if the strengths of 9 nodes all lie between 0.80 and 0.90, the z-scores still spread from about −1.5 to +1.5, and the differences look large in the plot. So a paper should also give the raw values (in a supplementary table, for example), so that readers can judge how large the differences are on the actual scale.</p>")]
FAQS["7.1"] = [
("With 3 waves, can I combine T1→T2 and T2→T3 and estimate one network?", "<p>Yes, but know the cost. One way is to stack the two intervals on top of each other and estimate once from 2N rows. Each edge then gets more information, but the same person appears twice, so the rows are not independent: cross-validation folds and bootstrap resampling should both use the person as the unit, or the standard errors will be too small. This course first estimates the two intervals separately (first part of Lesson 7.5) and uses the differences between them to check replication; the full procedure of Wysocki et al. (2025) instead puts equality constraints on the same path in both intervals in SEM, tests whether \"the two intervals are the same\" holds, and only then combines them (Lesson 7.5, R/L7-5b.R).</p>")]
FAQS["7.2"] = [
("Social Work Connection", "<p>Choosing a λ rule is like setting an alarm threshold: a low threshold gives more false alarms but rarely misses anything; a high threshold gives fewer false alarms but may miss real problems. Different research aims call for different trade-offs.</p>")]
FAQS["7.3"] = [
("Social Work Connection", "<p>When you report to an agency, you can show only the 5–8 strongest edges, labeled with the items' names in plain language, and put the full plot in an appendix. But \"strongest\" does not mean \"reliable\": in this lesson's example, only 2 of the top 5 edges are edges in the within-person truth. Give each edge's bootstrap interval as well (Lesson 8.2), and interpret only edges with stable intervals.</p>")]
FAQS["7.4"] = [
("Social Work Connection", "<p>If \"Fatigue\" has the highest in-EI, fatigue six months later is predicted by several earlier problems: it is where various difficulties \"converge\". At follow-up, fatigue may be a signal worth monitoring; whether it is a target worth intervening on is a separate question that needs evidence on direction and from intervention studies.</p>")]
FAQS["8.1"] = [
("Does a larger number of bootstrap resamples (B) make the interval narrower?", "<p>No. B determines the Monte Carlo error: with only 200 resamples, a different seed may move the interval's endpoints in the second decimal place; with 1000 or more, the endpoints settle down. How wide the interval is depends on the sample size N and the sampling variability of the statistic, and no B, however large, makes it narrower. So the problem with too small a B is \"unreliable endpoints\", not \"too wide an interval\"; once B is large enough, adding more only costs time. A few hundred resamples are usually enough for a standard error; for the 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles, use 1000 or more.</p>")]
FAQS["8.2"] = [
("Can I use the bootstrap intervals as significance tests for edges and count the \"significant edges\"?", "<p>No. The coverage of bootstrap intervals for regularized estimates is not guaranteed (point 3 of this lesson's \"three points for interpretation\"), they cannot be converted into p-values, and so \"the number of edges whose interval excludes 0\" is not \"the number of significant edges\". You can report that number in a paper as a summary of accuracy, but word it as \"interval excludes 0\", not \"significant\". If you need a test for single edges, use the full procedure introduced in Lesson 7.5 (p-values after unpenalized re-estimation; the capstone runs it as a required sensitivity analysis in Lesson 10.2).</p>")]
FAQS["7.6"] = [
("My items are 1–4 Likert items. Can I treat them as continuous for invariance testing?", "<p>With 5 or more categories and distributions that are not too skewed, treating items as continuous with MLR is common practice. With only 2–4 categories, or strongly skewed distributions, it is more appropriate to treat items as ordered categories (lavaan's <code>ordered = </code> with WLSMV estimation), and the constraints then apply to thresholds instead of intercepts. The course items have only 4 categories, so strictly they belong to the second case; this lesson treats them as continuous to stay consistent with Lessons 5.2 and 7.5 and to keep the \"intercept = zero point\" intuition clear. That is a simplification. The original paper on the coaches data (6 categories) used the ordered-category approach. In your own paper, choose by the number of categories and the shape of the distributions, and give your reasons in the methods section.</p>"),
("Social Work Connection", "<p>Agencies often use the same scale to compare groups: local residents and migrants, different language versions of a questionnaire, before and after a service. Any comparison of \"higher or lower\" assumes scalar invariance. When you see a report that \"group X scores higher\", first ask: does this item mean the same thing to people in both groups?</p>")]
FAQS["7.7"] = [
("Can I compare three or more groups?", "<p>You can compare them in pairs (a permutation test for each pair), but with more comparisons you again have to deal with multiple testing. Or decide on the single most important comparison beforehand and write it into your analysis plan.</p>"),
("Social Work Connection", "<p>\"Does this service work equally well for men and women?\" is a question agencies are often asked. Network comparison can tell you whether the links between symptoms differ between groups, for example whether loneliness and depressed mood are more closely linked in one group, which may point to different service priorities. But this assumes the samples are large enough and the groups were planned in advance, not picked after looking at the data.</p>")]
FAQS["8.3"] = [
("What if CS is low?", "<p>① Don't interpret that index's ranking; report only the values. ② Consider a larger sample. ③ Use fewer nodes or combine redundant items. ④ Say so in the limitations. Don't quietly switch to an index with a higher CS without reporting the original one; that too is a researcher degree of freedom (Lesson 9.2).</p>"),
("Why is in-EI more stable than out-EI in the course data?", "<p>One possible explanation: in-EI is the \"column sum\" of the coefficients of a single LASSO regression, while out-EI is a \"row sum\" that adds up coefficients from 9 different regressions, each with its own λ and noise. In the course data the CS of in-EI is {{V:cs8_in}} ({{V:cs10_in}} in the separate run of Lesson 10.2), while out-EI's is only {{V:cs8_out}}. But this is not a general rule; other data may differ, and you have to check.</p>")]
FAQS["9.1"] = [
("A paper reports no CS-coefficient and does not share its data. How should I read its centrality conclusions?", "<p>Only as results whose stability was never checked. You can make a few quick judgments: the number of nodes and N/p (this lesson's \"minimal math\"), whether the intervals of the strongest edges are very wide, and how far the top node is ahead of the second. These only point to risk; they cannot replace CS. In a literature review, flag the centrality conclusions of such studies separately; as a reviewer, ask the authors to run the case-dropping bootstrap and report CS.</p>")]
FAQS["10.1"] = [
("Can I use my own agency's data?", "<p>Yes, but you need ethics approval, informed consent and the agency's permission, and the data must be de-identified. Until all of that is in place, work through the whole process with the course's simulated data.</p>")]
FAQS["10.2"] = [
("My results on the simulated data don't match the answer. Did I do something wrong?", "<p>Not necessarily. First check which answer you are comparing with. In the course data, the <code>lambda.min</code> estimate correlates {{V:rec72_min_pop_r}} with the population target (true_B_population.csv) and {{V:rec72_min_win_r}} with the within-person truth (true_B_within.csv). The first gap comes from finite-sample noise, false positives and LASSO shrinkage; it appears even when the method is applied perfectly, and it shows what the method can achieve at N = 800. The second gap has one more layer: the population target of a standard CLPN is not a within-person process to begin with, and no sample size removes that (Lesson 7.2).</p>")]
for _k, _v in FAQS.items():
    if _k in L:
        L[_k]["intuition"] = L[_k]["intuition"] + FAQ(_v)
# Extra interactive widgets (so every lesson has a hands-on visualization)
L["5.2"]["intuition"] += "<h3>Interactive review: the CLPM path diagram</h3><p>Before you fit the lavaan model, use the diagram below to confirm which paths you are estimating: switch the autoregressive (AR) and cross-lagged (CL) paths on and off to see which arrow each parameter of the model stands for.</p>" + W("clpm", title="CLPM path diagram: switch autoregressive / cross-lagged paths on and off (they match the regression lines of the lavaan model syntax)")
L["6.4"]["intuition"] += "<h3>Interactive review: how the penalty changes the estimated network</h3><p>estimateNetwork(default = \"EBICglasso\") tries a series of λ values behind the scenes and picks one by EBIC. Drag the slider below and watch weak edges disappear one by one as λ grows: this is what EBICglasso does \"behind the scenes\".</p>" + W("lasso", title="Behind EBICglasso: change the LASSO penalty λ and watch the network become sparser")
L["9.2"]["intuition"] += "<h3>Interactive demonstration: what problem 2 looks like</h3><p>The second problem in the list is \"mixing up between-person and within-person\". The widget below compares the two answer keys for the same course data: the within-person truth (true_B_within) and the population target that a standard CLPN actually estimates (true_B_population); Lesson 7.2 explains the difference. Switch between them and watch how the out-EI ranking changes.</p>" + W("clpn", mode="ei", compare="1", which="pop", title="Within-person truth vs. the population target of a standard CLPN: does the centrality ranking change?") + "<h3>Interactive demonstration: problems 3 and 7 (centrality stability and sample size)</h3>" + W("cs", title="Review: case-dropping and the CS-coefficient")
